{"name":"Chinook Salmon","hex":"#c8987e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chinook salmon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Oncorhynchus tshawytscha, the largest species in the Pacific salmon group."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chinook_salmon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chinook salmon","blocks":[{"text":"The Chinook salmon is the largest and most valuable species of Pacific salmon. Its common name is derived from the Chinookan peoples. Other vernacular names for the species include king salmon, quinnat salmon, tsumen, spring salmon, blackmouth, and tyee salmon. The scientific species name is based on the Russian common name chavycha (чавыча)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Chinook_Salmon_Adult_Male.jpg/330px-Chinook_Salmon_Adult_Male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinook_salmon"},{"title":"Salmon","blocks":[{"text":"Salmon are any of several commercially important species of ray-finned fish that can live in either freshwater or saltwater from the genera Salmo and Oncorhynchus of the family Salmonidae, native to tributaries of the North Atlantic (Salmo) and North Pacific (Oncorhynchus) basins. Salmon is a colloquial or common name used for fish in this group, but is not a scientific name. Other closely related fish in the same family include trout, char, grayling, whitefish, lenok and taimen, all coldwater fish of the subarctic and cooler temperate regions with some sporadic endorheic populations in Central Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Salmo_salar.jpg/330px-Salmo_salar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salmon"},{"title":"Salmon (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Salmon is a warm color ranging from light orange to pink, named after the color of salmon flesh."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/SockeyeMaleCloseup_%2814522094556%29.jpg/330px-SockeyeMaleCloseup_%2814522094556%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salmon_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/OncorhynchusTschawytscha2.jpg/500px-OncorhynchusTschawytscha2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/OncorhynchusTschawytscha2.jpg/500px-OncorhynchusTschawytscha2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A male Chinook in his spawning phase","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOncorhynchusTschawytscha2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/King_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg/500px-King_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/King_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg/1280px-King_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/King_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg/500px-King_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chinook salmon tail, showing distinctive combination of black spots and silver","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKing_salmon_tail_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/King_salmon_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg/500px-King_salmon_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/King_salmon_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg/1280px-King_salmon_by_Nick_Longrich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp